 "location": {
  "name": "Zhangjiajie National Forest Park",
  "wiki": "Zhangjiajie_National_Forest_Park",
  "country": "China",
  "region": "Wulingyuan, Zhangjiajie, Hunan Province",
  "lat": 29.35,
  "lon": 110.4353,
  "tagline": "A forest of sandstone pillars so otherworldly that one of them was renamed after Avatar.",
  "intro": "Zhangjiajie became China's first national forest park in 1982, a maze of thousands of slender quartz-sandstone pillars rising out of mist-filled ravines in northwest Hunan. It is the heart of the Wulingyuan Scenic Area, a UNESCO World Heritage Site since 1992. Cable cars, a monorail and a cliffside glass elevator carry visitors up to the summits.",
  "stats": [
   {
    "label": "Established",
    "value": "1982 (China's first national forest park)"
   },
   {
    "label": "UNESCO World Heritage",
    "value": "Since 1992 (Wulingyuan)"
   },
   {
    "label": "Area",
    "value": "4,810 hectares"
   },
   {
    "label": "Bailong Elevator",
    "value": "326 m tall"
   }
  ],
  "facts": [
   "In January 2010 the pillar known as the Southern Sky Column was officially renamed 'Avatar Hallelujah Mountain' after the film's floating mountains, which designers reportedly based partly on this landscape.",
   "The Bailong Elevator, opened in 2002, rises 326 metres up a cliff face and is billed as the world's tallest outdoor lift; each of its three glass cars carries up to 50 people in under two minutes.",
   "The pillars look like limestone karst but aren't: they are quartz sandstone, carved mainly by physical erosion such as winter ice expanding in cracks and plant roots prying rock apart.",
   "The nearby Zhangjiajie Grand Canyon Glass Bridge, 430 metres long and 300 metres above the canyon floor, opened in August 2016 and had to close just 13 days later because of the crowds.",
   "The area is also a UNESCO Global Geopark, listed in 2004, and its pillar forms show up in many ancient Chinese paintings."
  ],
  "bestTime": "Spring and autumn, when the weather is mild and mist drifts between the peaks. Summer is rainy and cloudy, and cable cars can close in winter. Avoid China's National Day week (Oct 1–8) and May 1–5.",
  "gettingThere": "Fly from New York to a Chinese hub such as Beijing or Shanghai, then connect to Zhangjiajie Hehua airport. From downtown Zhangjiajie, buses to the park and Wulingyuan run about every 10 minutes and take around 40 minutes.",
  "tip": "The main ticket is good for 4 days, so stay in Wulingyuan right by the park, and use the quieter Yangjiajie or Tianzi Mountain entrances (bring your passport for Tianzi Mountain)."
 },
